# PR write-ups

When finishing a **feature branch**, **bugfix with a PR**, or when the user asks to **commit / push / open a PR** for substantive work (not typo-only or drive-by one-liners):

1. **Create or update** a Markdown file under `docs/PR_WriteUps/`.
2. **Name** it `PR_<TOPIC>.md` in `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` (e.g. `PR_DASHBOARD_JQL_EPIC_METRICS.md`).
3. **Reuse** an existing write-up for the same topic/branch when possible; do not invent parallel files for the same change set.
4. **Keep the write-up current** when scope on that branch changes (add sections; do not leave stale claims).
5. **Commit the write-up with the branch** (these files are tracked in git — do not recreate them as local-only drafts).
6. When creating a GitHub PR, **use the write-up as the PR body** (or as the Summary / Test plan source). Prefer the tracked file over inventing a separate narrative.

## Required sections

Mirror existing files in `docs/PR_WriteUps/` (see `PR_CREATE_ISSUE_FIXES.md`):

| Section | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| `# PR: …` title | One-line label for the PR |
| `## Summary` | 1–3 sentences: what and why |
| `## Problem` | Symptom → root cause (table when helpful) |
| `## Changes` | Grouped by area/path; concrete, not speculative |
| `## Test plan` | Checklist of manual / automated checks |
| `## Files touched` | Optional table of main paths |

## Do not

- Skip the write-up when opening a multi-commit feature PR because the chat already “explained” it.
- Put secrets, tokens, or PII in write-ups.
- Overwrite an unrelated write-up for a different feature.
- Create a write-up for trivial-only commits (formatting, single-line typo) unless the user asks.

